WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.188 --> 00:00:05.069
Thanks to Brilliant for supporting this episode
of Journey to the Microcosmos.

00:00:05.069 --> 00:00:11.237
Go to Brilliant.org/microcosmos to learn how
you can take your STEM skills to the next level!

00:00:32.618 --> 00:00:36.981
It’s often said that one person’s trash
is another person’s treasure.

00:00:36.981 --> 00:00:42.402
And surely there is no greater proof of that
than the home of our master of microscopes,

00:00:42.402 --> 00:00:43.510
James.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:48.770
All along the windowsills and bookshelves
are jars and tanks full of samples gathered

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:52.150
from ponds, lakes, and oceans.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:58.480
And even his cabinets and drawers and bathroom
hold stockpiles of what he has found.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.942
To someone who may not know any better, James’
house probably seems messy.

00:01:03.291 --> 00:01:04.952
Very, very messy.

00:01:04.952 --> 00:01:06.561
But we know better.

00:01:06.561 --> 00:01:13.342
We know that his unusual decor is a testament
to his dedication to exploring the microcosmos,

00:01:13.342 --> 00:01:18.341
countless little worlds that James has collected
and taken care of.

00:01:18.341 --> 00:01:23.102
And most importantly, James doesn’t have
a problem sharing space with his samples.

00:01:23.102 --> 00:01:24.102
Scratch that.

00:01:24.102 --> 00:01:26.003
There actually is one problem...

00:01:26.003 --> 00:01:27.250
The snails.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.740
Whenever James brings home his latest collection,
his goal is to be able to watch the organisms

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.299
inside for a very long time.

00:01:38.299 --> 00:01:43.430
That’s why he takes such care to give them
the sun and water and nutrition that each

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:48.783
tiny little world needs so that he can watch
the populations of algae and hydra 

00:01:48.783 --> 00:01:51.000
and so much more grow.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.750
But aquatic snails are the slow-moving Godzillas
of these worlds, dining on just about anything

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.560
that is in their path.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:03.605
They chomp away at the unfortunate creatures
that get in their way, 

00:02:03.605 --> 00:02:07.224
leaving a path of destruction in their wake.

00:02:07.224 --> 00:02:10.060
And worse, they reproduce like bunny rabbits.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.520
So with very little effort, a small snail
problem can become a very big snail problem,

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:21.310
a landscape overrun with a creature that simply
does not care about the human whose treasure

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.822
it’s devouring into trash.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:30.850
So a sight like this one should be a crisis
for a master of microscopes.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:35.420
Aquatic snails lay their eggs in a gelatinous
mass, which can hold hundreds of little guys

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:39.770
like these squirming in their developing bodies
and shells.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:45.056
Depending on the species, you might find these
sacks of eggs floating in water or stuck to plants.

00:02:45.056 --> 00:02:47.900
Or, maybe even on the wall of an aquarium.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:53.270
But as terrifying as the prospect of a future
filled with hundreds of new snails ready to

00:02:53.270 --> 00:03:00.210
eat up all of James’ samples is, even he
has to admit that…these are kind of adorable.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:05.480
They’re both squishy and iridescent, an
uncommon pairing made all the cuter with their

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.480
beady little eyes.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:12.540
The snail eggs that James finds usually develop
pretty fast, sometimes hatching within a few

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.890
days of when he stumbles upon them.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:19.090
But this time he was lucky, he was able to
catch them right before they hatched so the

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.510
embryos were fully visible.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:24.920
But while they might be adorable now, they
won’t stay that way.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:30.490
They will grow quickly, the small pearly iridescent
shell building into the larger encasements

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:32.580
that house mature snails.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.630
But let’s be upfront about something.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:39.600
There are thousands and thousands of species
of snail, and we at Journey to the Microcosmos

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.650
don’t know enough to be able to identify
exactly what species we’re looking at.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.850
So we’re speaking very generally today.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:53.140
Snails are members of the Mollusca phylum,
which includes oysters, clams, octopuses, and squids.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:57.570
And while not all of those creatures make
shells, the ones that do build primarily with

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:02.910
calcium carbonate, a hard mineral that protects
the softer animal within.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:07.890
As we’ve seen, the building of those shells
begins before the snails are even born.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.560
And those shells will remain with the 
snail until they die.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:17.690
As an embryo, the snail grows a protoconch,
which will lay a sort of foundation for the future shell.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:22.870
And after hatching, construction continues
as an organ called the mantle begins to secrete

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:26.310
layers of calcium carbonate that then crystallize.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:31.260
As more layers are added, the shell’s characteristic
spiral begins to take shape.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:36.480
Interestingly, snail shells almost always
spiral clockwise.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:41.170
But they can, on rare occasions, produce a
counter-clockwise spiral, a mutation that

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.580
researchers were able to trace to a single gene.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:49.380
And if the shell is broken during the snail’s
lifetime, it can produce more calcium carbonate

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.280
through its mantle to seal up the cracks.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:57.160
It’s clear though from the different patterns
and colors we can see on these shells that

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.820
there’s more to shell-building than just
calcium carbonate.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:05.770
The outside of the shells are encased in a
layer called periostracum, made up of proteins,

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:12.320
carbohydrates, fats…and pigments that will
eventually come to define the snail’s appearance.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.160
Shells may be a snail’s most obvious feature,
but there are plenty of other fascinating

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:17.710
things to see as well.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:22.630
Like these grains wiggling around inside a
recently deceased snail.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:27.830
The organ that holds them is called a statocyst,
and it helps the snail understand just how

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:31.410
its body is situated in the space around them.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:37.810
As the snail moves, the grains—or statoliths—bump
up against the walls of the statocyst, brushing

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:42.890
against little sensory hairs that send information
to the snail’s nervous system so it can

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.690
process its shifting position.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:50.460
And that strange texture you see in the middle
of the screen, which looks like a weird footprint

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:55.659
that’s actually a dead snail’s tongue, or more
accurately, its radula.

00:05:55.659 --> 00:05:58.710
Radulas are a structure particular to mollusks.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:02.870
And while we originally called it a tongue,
it’s perhaps more appropriate to refer to

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:10.660
the radula as a membrane covered in rows and
rows of teeth that works with the snail’s flexible jaw.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:16.220
As the snail moves, it can use its radula
to scrape up algae or cut up plants.

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:21.630
Which means that this weird membrane full
of teeth, this wondrous utensil that helps

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:28.900
the snail eat as it slowly ambles along the
world—this is James’ enemy.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:34.790
This is the nozzle attached to the unwelcome
vacuum cleaner that digests and clears up

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:40.320
the jars and tanks that he does not want to
be cleared.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:43.332
But as we said: one person’s trash.

00:06:43.332 --> 00:06:44.990
Another person’s treasure.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:50.370
There are many aquarium owners who seek
out snails precisely because they will clear

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:55.500
out algae and other small organisms or waste
that take over their tank.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:01.870
Snails serve an important ecological function
in these cases, maintaining an environment

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:06.261
that supports the growth of 
other good organisms.

00:07:06.261 --> 00:07:11.970
So, as the old saying goes, one person’s
slow moving Godzilla is another’s fish tank

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:12.890
vacuum cleaner.

00:07:16.754 --> 00:07:21.981
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

